  "unit": "Unit 20 - Circles",
  "description": "Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 20 - Circles for SAT Math preparation.",
  "duration": 45,
  "content": "<p><strong>Unit 20 - Circles — Title &amp; Goal</strong><br />Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 20 - Circles for SAT Math preparation.</p>\n\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n<p>Read the idea and scan the rules. Focus on how the symbols behave.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circumference:</li>\n</ul>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>\n<ul>\n<li>Area:</li>\n</ul>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p>Follow the steps carefully, then try to reproduce them without looking.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br/>\nIf <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, then:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>49</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>\n<h3>Quick practice</h3>\n<p>Try these on your own. Check your steps, not just the final answer.</p>\n<p>1) Find the circumference of a circle with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.\n2) Find the area of a circle with diameter <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>.\n3) In a circle of radius <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, find the length of an arc subtended by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>72</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.\n<h3>Next idea</h3>\n<p>Build on the previous idea. Notice what changes and what stays the same.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Arc length is a portion of the circumference:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mi>·</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>(or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math> in radians).</p>\n<h3>Another example</h3>\n<p>Compare this approach to the previous one. What’s the same? What’s different?</p>\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br/>\nFind the arc length of a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> arc in a circle of radius <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>90</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>360</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>·</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>\n<hr />\n<p>Sector area is a portion of the total area:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mtext>sector</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mi>·</mi><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>\n<p>(or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mtext>sector</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math> in radians).</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br/>\nFind the area of a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> sector with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>360</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>·</mi><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>\n<hr />\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central angle</strong>: vertex at the circle’s center, equals arc measure.  </li>\n<li><strong>Inscribed angle</strong>: vertex on the circle, equals <strong>half the arc</strong> it intercepts.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br />If arc <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>80</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>, then:</p><ul><li>Central angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>80</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></li><li>Inscribed angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></li></ul>\n<hr />\n<p>General form:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the center and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> the radius.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 5</strong><br/>\nEquation of circle centered at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></math>\n<hr />\n<ul>\n<li>Inscribed angle = half arc is very common.  </li>\n<li>For arc/sector problems, always check if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math> is in degrees or radians.  </li>\n<li>If you see <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> terms, think “equation of a circle.”  </li>\n<li>Don’t forget diameter <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> when needed.  </li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p>1) Find the circumference of a circle with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.<br/>\n2) Find the area of a circle with diameter <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>.<br/>\n3) In a circle of radius <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, find the length of an arc subtended by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>72</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.<br/>\n4) Find the area of a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> sector with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.<br/>\n5) In a circle, arc <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the inscribed angle subtending that arc.<br/>\n6) Write the equation of a circle centered at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> with radius <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr />\n<p>1) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>2) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>36</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>3) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>72</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>360</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>·</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>4) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>90</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>360</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>·</mi><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>64</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>5) Inscribed angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>50</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>6) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<h3>More practice</h3>\n<p>Keep going—aim for accuracy first, then speed.</p>\n<h3>Mixed practice set</h3>\n<p>These mix everything above. Time yourself and reflect on any mistakes.</p>\n4) Find the area of a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> sector with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.\n5) In a circle, arc <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the inscribed angle subtending that arc.\n6) Write the equation of a circle centered at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> with radius <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.",
